{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"agent_message","text":"# Evidence-collection handoff: Joe Biden — United States — 2023\n\n**Job:** `dossier:2023-top20-usa-tha-repair-v1:2023:USA:15811`  \n**Ruler:** Joe Biden  \n**Period:** 1 January–31 December 2023  \n**Research status:** substantive compact pass completed; not a final score or judgment  \n**File status:** the requested notebook and manifest could not be written because this worker’s filesystem was read-only. The material below is the authoritative handoff for parent persistence.\n\n## 1. Execution and source-access audit\n\nI began with the supplied local package and did not re-fetch Freedom House, V-Dem, WDI, CPI, RSF, PTS, or SIPRI structured data.\n\nWeb discovery used:\n\n- MiniMax `web_search`, with distinct broad, chapter-specific, institutional-source, and adverse-evidence queries.\n- `fetch` for underlying pages where access was available.\n- Brave context was attempted but returned quota/rate-limit errors and furnished no evidence.\n- Reuters discovery results were useful for candidate identification, but direct Reuters fetches were blocked by `robots.txt`. Reuters snippets therefore remain **gateway-only/discovery context**, not defensible opened-page evidence.\n- Congress.gov’s CRS page returned HTTP 403 and remains a rejected inaccessible candidate.\n- Some PDFs—most notably the DOJ Hur report and Brookings economic-policy transcript—were returned as raw PDF bytes rather than extractable text. Opened HTML summaries or exact searchable source snippets were used instead; the raw PDFs were not counted as independently extracted claim units unless a usable locator was available.\n\nThe strongest opened source organizations were:\n\n1. Arms Control Association\n2. United Nations\n3. Human Rights Watch\n4. American Civil Liberties Union\n5. International IDEA\n6. U.S. Census Bureau\n7. Associated Press\n8. FactCheck.org\n9. PolitiFact\n10. Brookings Institution\n\nThe source mix includes official/intergovernmental records, NGOs, reputable media, fact-checking organizations, and policy research. Local structured priors remain a separate source family.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Global evidence register\n\nThese are stable provisional entries suitable for conversion into `research-materials.md`. Canonical fact keys deliberately distinguish materially different claims even when sourced to the same URL.\n\n## Local structured context\n\n### L001 — U.S. military-spending context\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:2B.1|LF001-LF004|USA-2023-military-spending`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Source/locator:** `local-prior:2B.1`\n- **Publisher/type:** SIPRI local structured prior; structured dataset\n- **Confidence:** high\n- **Claim:** In 2023, supplied SIPRI facts report military expenditure of `943032.7844566408` in the source field’s constant-USD scale, per-capita spending of `2674.6899419822926`, military spending equal to `0.03293652410258671` of GDP, and `0.08748080061220297` of government spending.\n- **Locator:** LF001–LF004, exact supplied local-prior locator\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** country-year budget context; does not establish that Biden preferred force, acted aggressively, or used spending responsibly.\n- **Limitations:** supplied LF001 label does not state whether its numeric unit is millions; retain the exact value without silently converting it.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.8; contextual for 2B.1, 2B.2, 2B.10.\n\n### L002 — U.S. physical-integrity and repression context\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:3B.1|LF005-LF010|USA-2023-physical-integrity`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Source/locator:** `local-prior:3B.1`\n- **Publisher/type:** V-Dem and PTS local structured priors\n- **Confidence:** high for measured country-year fields\n- **Claim:** The package supplies 2023 V-Dem civil-society repression `1.915`, extrajudicial-killings `2.857`, physical-integrity liberties `0.949`, private civil liberties `0.901`, plus PTS Amnesty and HRW scores of `3.0`.\n- **Locator:** LF005–LF010\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** country conditions; does not by itself identify presidential direction, federal versus state responsibility, incidence, or remedy.\n- **Mappings:** contextual across 3B.1–3B.10.\n\n### L003 — Democratic institutions and political-freedom context\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:4B.1|LF011-LF025|USA-2023-democracy-freedom`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Source/locator:** `local-prior:4B.1`\n- **Publisher/type:** V-Dem, Freedom House, and RSF local structured priors\n- **Confidence:** high for the supplied fields\n- **Claim:** The package supplies strong 2023 country-level measures of electoral democracy, association, expression, judicial and legislative constraints, liberal democracy, suffrage, rule of law, and related fields; Freedom House political-rights/civil-liberties values are both `2.0`; RSF rank is 45 with score 71.22.\n- **Locator:** LF011–LF025\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** inherited and country-level institutional context, not affirmative proof that Biden personally protected or strengthened freedom.\n- **Mappings:** contextual across 4B; LF011/LF024 also context for 7B and 8B.\n\n### L004 — Economic scale and income context\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:5B.1|LF026-LF028|USA-2023-economic-level`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Source/locator:** `local-prior:5B.1`\n- **Publisher/type:** World Bank WDI local structured prior\n- **Confidence:** high for direct fields\n- **Claim:** Supplied 2023 facts report GDP per capita of `81032.262117545`, total GDP of `27292170793214.4`, and population of `336806231`.\n- **Locator:** LF026–LF028\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** country level and scale; cannot establish distribution, policy competence, or Biden’s causal contribution.\n- **Mappings:** context for 5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.6, 5B.8, 5B.10.\n\n### L005 — Corruption and rule-of-law context\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:7B.1|LF029-LF032|USA-2023-corruption`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Source/locator:** `local-prior:7B.1`\n- **Publisher/type:** V-Dem and Transparency International local structured priors\n- **Confidence:** high for measured national fields\n- **Claim:** The package supplies political-corruption index `0.055`, CPI score `69`, executive corruption `0.013`, and public-sector corruption `0.021`, alongside LF011 accountability and LF024 rule of law.\n- **Locator:** LF029–LF032, with LF011 and LF024\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** national institutional context; cannot prove Biden’s personal honesty, family-interest separation, candor, or acceptance of scrutiny.\n- **Mappings:** context for 7B and inherited-capacity context for 8B.\n\n---\n\n## Internet evidence\n\n### E001 — Biden administration’s June 2023 arms-control program\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.armscontrol.org/events/2023-06/remarks-national-security-advisor-jake-sullivan-arms-control-association-aca-annual|sections-US-ready-engage-Russia-China-P5|Biden-administration-arms-control-offer`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Remarks by National Security Advisor Jake Sullivan for the Arms Control Association Annual Forum”\n- **Publisher/date:** Arms Control Association, reproducing White House remarks; 2 June 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** primary official speech reproduced by specialist NGO; **medium-high** for declared policy and formal offer, lower for implementation success\n- **Claim:** The administration declared willingness to remain within New START’s central limits if Russia did likewise; engage Russia immediately on post-2026 controls; engage China without preconditions; and pursue P5 launch notification, crisis communications, human control, and policy transparency.\n- **Locator/excerpt:** Sections beginning “It is in neither of our countries’ interests…” and “The United States is ready to engage Russia now…”; the speech lists “maintaining a ‘human-in-the-loop’,” crisis channels, and launch notification.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** Sullivan explicitly presented the program under “the President’s leadership”; strong administration-level, moderate direct-personal attribution.\n- **Mitigation/contrary:** It was a policy declaration; E002 shows incomplete follow-through.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.1, 1B.3, 1B.4, 1B.7, 1B.8, 1B.9, 1B.10; also 2B.1 and 2B.7.\n\n### E002 — Delay in converting arms-control speech into a formal proposal\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.armscontrol.org/blog/2023-09/nuclear-disarmament-monitor|opening-paragraphs-US-proposal|no-formal-written-proposal-three-months`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Three Months Later, Still No U.S. Arms Control Proposal to Russia”\n- **Publisher/date:** Arms Control Association Nuclear Disarmament Monitor; 12 September 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** specialist NGO analysis; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Three months after Sullivan’s speech, the United States had not sent Russia a formal written proposal, according to the report; former official Thomas Countryman called failure to take the next step inconsistent with the urgency of the speech.\n- **Locator/excerpt:** Opening two paragraphs and paragraph quoting Countryman.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** administration implementation gap; Biden’s personal role is not established.\n- **Contrary:** NSC said the proposal had been privately conveyed and Russia appeared unwilling to engage.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.1, 1B.4, 1B.8, 1B.10; 8B.2, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.8.\n\n### E003 — Nuclear posture included continuing restraint but also forward-capability concerns\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.armscontrol.org/blog/2023-09/nuclear-disarmament-monitor|section-Lakenheath-surety-mission|possible-return-US-nuclear-mission-UK`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** Nuclear Disarmament Monitor, section on RAF Lakenheath\n- **Publisher/date:** Arms Control Association; September 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** specialist NGO synthesis citing FAS analysis and Air Force budget documents; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Air Force FY2024 budget documents indicated preparation for a potential nuclear “surety” mission at RAF Lakenheath, coinciding with B61-12 deployment and nuclear-capable F-35A basing; Russia publicly characterized a return as escalation.\n- **Locator:** Section discussing Lakenheath and “potential Surety mission.”\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 planning\n- **Attribution:** administration/military posture, but no direct evidence that Biden personally ordered the move.\n- **Contrary:** Capability and alliance posture are not themselves proof of recklessness; Russian characterization is interested-party evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.1, 1B.5, 1B.10; contextual 2B.8.\n\n### E004 — U.S. veto of Gaza humanitarian-pauses resolution\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://news.un.org/en/story/2023/10/1142507|sections-vote-US-rejection|US-veto-humanitarian-pauses-resolution`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Israel-Gaza crisis: US vetoes Security Council resolution”\n- **Publisher/date:** UN News; 18 October 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** intergovernmental official record/report; **high** for the vote and stated rationale\n- **Claim:** The United States cast the sole veto against a Brazilian-led resolution supported by 12 Security Council members and calling for humanitarian pauses. The U.S. ambassador said it omitted Israel’s right of self-defense and asserted that direct diplomacy could save lives.\n- **Locator:** Opening vote tally and “US rejection” section.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** formal U.S. executive foreign-policy act during Biden’s presidency; strong administration attribution, moderate direct personal attribution.\n- **Contrary:** U.S. explanation emphasized self-defense, civilian protection, and Biden’s on-the-ground diplomacy; the resolution did not call for a full ceasefire.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.1, 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.7, 2B.9, 2B.10.\n\n### E005 — Mixed U.S. international human-rights record\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/united-states|opening-summary-foreign-policy|military-assistance-human-rights-abusing-states`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “World Report 2024: United States”\n- **Publisher/date:** Human Rights Watch; 2024 report covering 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** rights NGO; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** HRW found that the United States used targeted sanctions against abusers and supported international justice mechanisms, including the ICC investigation concerning Ukraine, but undermined its human-rights commitment by providing military assistance to abusive states.\n- **Locator:** Opening summary, second paragraph.\n- **Period fit:** direct retrospective assessment of 2023\n- **Attribution:** Biden-administration foreign policy; specific arms decisions require further item-level corroboration.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.9, 2B.10; contextual 7B.8.\n\n### E006 — Calm/de-escalatory great-power management, with mixed execution\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.brookings.edu/articles/bidens-unspectacular-but-solid-national-security-record/|opening-assessment-and-Ukraine-section|Biden-de-escalatory-but-slow-weapons-decisions`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Biden’s unspectacular but solid national security record”\n- **Publisher/date:** Brookings Institution; 30 January 2024\n- **Source type/confidence:** policy commentary; **medium**\n- **Claim:** The author characterizes Biden as calm and de-escalatory toward major powers and credits historic Ukraine assistance, while criticizing repeated 2023 hesitation over major weapons transfers and acknowledging mixed policy on Iran, North Korea, debt, and other issues.\n- **Locator:** Opening assessment and “Ukraine and Russia” section.\n- **Period fit:** retrospective assessment immediately after 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct presidential foreign-policy assessment, but interpretive rather than adjudicative.\n- **Contrary:** The article explicitly notes Congress’s central role and cautions against attributing the entire U.S. record to Biden.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.2, 1B.8, 1B.10; 2B.1, 2B.3, 2B.7, 2B.10; 8B.8, 8B.9.\n\n### E007 — Federal police-reform implementation and remaining loopholes\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.aclu.org/press-releases/aclu-acknowledges-improvements-to-doj-racial-profiling-policy-but-says-far-more-is-needed|implementation-policies-and-racial-profiling-sections|policing-EO-implementation-mixed`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “On Anniversary of President Biden’s Policing Executive Order, ACLU Disappointed by Administration’s Failure to Fully Ban Discriminatory Profiling”\n- **Publisher/date:** ACLU; 25 May 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** civil-liberties NGO; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** More than 20 implementation policies, guidelines, and reports were released under Biden’s policing order. Federal policies limited chokeholds/deadly force, imposed duties to intervene and render aid, and expanded profiling protections, but significant national-security, intelligence, immigration, and implementation loopholes remained.\n- **Locator:** Opening through the sections listing profiling-policy changes and use-of-force requirements.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** strong executive-branch attribution through Biden’s order; effectiveness at state/local level remained uncertain.\n- **Contrary:** ACLU welcomed improvements but said the reforms failed to establish a full ban and required stronger implementation.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.5, 3B.7, 3B.8, 3B.10; 4B.7; 8B.2, 8B.6.\n\n### E008 — Expanded and privatized immigration detention\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.aclu.org/news/immigrants-rights/unchecked-growth-private-prison-corporations-and-immigration-detention-three-years-into-the-biden-administration|daily-detention-private-share-sections|ICE-detention-growth-private-facilities`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Private Prison Corporations and Immigration Detention, Three Years Into the Biden Administration”\n- **Publisher/date:** ACLU; 7 August 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** civil-liberties NGO using ICE/TRAC and corporate records; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Average ICE detention increased from 15,444 people at the start of Biden’s administration to 30,003 in July 2023, and 90.8% of detainees were held in privately owned or operated facilities. Biden’s 2021 private-prison order excluded ICE.\n- **Locator:** Sections “The Number of Immigrants Detained…” and “Nine Out of Ten…”\n- **Period fit:** exact July 2023\n- **Attribution:** strong for continued federal policy and exclusion of ICE; Congress also set detention appropriations.\n- **Contrary:** The administration phased out some DOJ private-prison contracts; immigration detention was not covered.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.5, 3B.7, 3B.10; 6B.3, 6B.7, 6B.8; 7B.8; 8B.6, 8B.10.\n\n### E009 — Asylum restrictions and risks to migrants\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/united-states|section-Immigrants-Rights-and-Border-Communities|Biden-2023-asylum-rule`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “World Report 2024: United States”\n- **Publisher/date:** Human Rights Watch; 2024, covering 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** rights NGO; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** After Title 42 expired, the administration replaced it with an asylum rule that HRW says exposed many asylum seekers to expedited removal, detention, prosecution, a five-year return bar, and prolonged danger while seeking CBP One appointments.\n- **Locator:** “Immigrants’ Rights and Border Communities” section.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 policy\n- **Attribution:** direct federal executive immigration policy, though courts and Congress constrained options.\n- **Contrary:** Administration framed the rule as border management and created parole/legal-pathway mechanisms; this source focuses on harms.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.5, 3B.7, 3B.9, 3B.10; 4B.7; 6B.3, 6B.6, 6B.8.\n\n### E010 — U.S. democratic resilience and structural restrictions\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.idea.int/gsod/2023/chapters/americas/case/united-states-of-america|voting-restrictions-and-disinformation-sections|US-2023-democracy-mixed`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “The state of democracy in the Americas — United States case”\n- **Publisher/date:** International IDEA; 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** intergovernmental democracy analysis; **high**\n- **Claim:** Elections remained an effective check, but the electoral college, voter suppression, and disinformation impaired political equality. Between January and May 2023, 11 states passed at least 13 restrictive voting laws, while expansive laws outnumbered restrictive ones. Courts continued to constrain discriminatory maps.\n- **Locator:** “Impact of the electoral college,” voting restrictions, “The risk of disinformation,” and countervailing-institutions sections.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** mostly inherited constitutional and state-level conditions, not personally Biden-directed.\n- **Contrary:** Elections and courts continued to operate as real constraints; expansive voting laws also passed.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.4, 4B.7, 4B.10.\n\n### E011 — Federal democracy protections alongside unresolved threats\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/united-states|opening-summary-and-Democracy-section|Biden-democracy-protection-context`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “World Report 2024: United States”\n- **Publisher/date:** Human Rights Watch; 2024, covering 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** NGO; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** HRW reported continued threats against election officials and disinformation, while a federal task force documented harassment and the Supreme Court reinforced voting-rights protections. Biden’s administration banned federal use of abusive commercial spyware.\n- **Locator:** Opening summary and “Democracy” section.\n- **Period fit:** direct 2023\n- **Attribution:** spyware ban and federal task force have executive links; court rulings and state conduct are independent of Biden.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.3, 3B.4, 3B.8; 4B.1, 4B.3, 4B.4, 4B.7, 4B.9, 4B.10.\n\n### E012 — Real wage improvement during 2023\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.bls.gov/opub/ted/2024/real-average-hourly-earnings-increased-1-4-percent-from-january-2023-to-january-2024.htm|headline-statistic|real-hourly-earnings-1.4-percent`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Real average hourly earnings increased 1.4 percent from January 2023 to January 2024”\n- **Publisher/date:** U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics; 26 February 2024\n- **Source type/confidence:** official statistics; **high** for the measured outcome\n- **Claim:** BLS reports inflation-adjusted average hourly earnings rose 1.4% between January 2023 and January 2024.\n- **Locator:** headline statistic/search-result locator; direct fetch was robots-blocked, so retain as **locator_missing/gateway-only unless the parent can open the BLS page**.\n- **Period fit:** nearly exact annual 2023 interval\n- **Attribution:** macro outcome only; Federal Reserve, reopening, labor supply, Congress, and external conditions are important alternative causes.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.3, 5B.8, 5B.9, 5B.10; 6B.10.\n- **Defensibility note:** keep out of strict final count until direct page text is persisted.\n\n### E013 — Mixed 2023 poverty outcomes\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-283.html|summary-bullets-official-and-SPM|2023-poverty-rates`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Poverty in the United States: 2023”\n- **Publisher/date:** U.S. Census Bureau; 10 September 2024\n- **Source type/confidence:** official statistics; **high**\n- **Claim:** In 2023, the official poverty rate fell 0.4 points to 11.1%, while the Supplemental Poverty Measure rose 0.5 points to 12.9%; SPM child poverty rose 1.3 points to 13.7%.\n- **Locator:** Summary bullets under “Official Poverty Measure” and “Supplemental Poverty Measure.”\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 outcome, published retrospectively\n- **Attribution:** outcome only. Tax policy, expiration of pandemic supports, Congress, inflation, and state policy complicate presidential attribution.\n- **Contrary:** Official and supplemental measures moved in opposite directions.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.8, 5B.9, 5B.10; 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.6, 6B.10.\n\n### E014 — HRW assessment of social and economic conditions\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/united-states|opening-summary-economic-justice|poverty-inequality-incarceration`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “World Report 2024: United States”\n- **Publisher/date:** Human Rights Watch; 2024, covering 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** NGO synthesis; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** HRW found poverty and inequality rose after pandemic-era support ended; incarceration increased; racial wealth gaps remained severe. It also notes the administration’s first-in-government investment in harm reduction and the FDA’s March 2023 authorization of over-the-counter naloxone.\n- **Locator:** Opening summary, “Economic Justice,” and “Drug Policy.”\n- **Period fit:** exact/near-period\n- **Attribution:** mixed—some federal policy, some congressional/state/inherited conditions.\n- **Contrary:** Harm-reduction and naloxone measures were affirmative federal welfare interventions.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.6, 3B.7, 3B.10; 5B.8, 5B.10; 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.5, 6B.6, 6B.8, 6B.10.\n\n### E015 — SAVE student-loan repayment plan\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.reuters.com/world/us/new-biden-student-loan-plan-cuts-payments-millions-white-house-says-2023-08-22/|search-result-summary|SAVE-plan-estimated-savings`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title:** “New Biden student loan plan cuts payments for millions”\n- **Publisher/date:** Reuters; 22 August 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** reputable wire; normally **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Reuters reported that the 2023 repayment changes were estimated to save a typical borrower about $1,000 annually and a typical public four-year graduate about $2,000.\n- **Locator:** Reuters discovery snippet only; direct fetch was robots-blocked.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023\n- **Attribution:** direct administration policy.\n- **Limitations:** **gateway_only**; not counted as defensible until article or underlying Education Department rule is opened.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.6, 5B.8; 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.8; 8B.8.\n\n### E016 — Biden’s 2023 public economic claims included truth and exaggeration\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.politifact.com/article/2023/feb/08/fact-checking-joe-bidens-2023-state-union-address/|job-creation-noncompete-infrastructure-sections|State-of-Union-claim-accuracy-mixed`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Fact-checking Joe Biden’s 2023 State of the Union address”\n- **Publisher/date:** PolitiFact; 8 February 2023, updated 13 February\n- **Source type/confidence:** professional fact-checking media; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Biden accurately cited roughly 12 million jobs added but overstated comparability with prior presidencies because recovery from pandemic losses and population size mattered. His fast-food noncompete example conflated noncompete and no-poach clauses, and some infrastructure and wildfire claims lacked adequate context or support.\n- **Locator:** Sections beginning “Thirty million workers…,” “12 million new jobs,” and wildfire/infrastructure sections.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 statement\n- **Attribution:** direct personal public statements.\n- **Contrary:** Several central claims had a factual core and were not wholly false.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.1, 5B.7; 7B.1, 7B.2, 7B.8, 7B.10; 8B.1, 8B.7.\n\n### E017 — Classified-documents cooperation and adverse findings\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://apnews.com/article/biden-hur-garland-classified-documents-836b99fe9cbef9ba7d32602f4928efec|opening-findings-and-case-distinctions|Hur-report-Biden-retention-no-charge`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Biden pushes back at special counsel claims that questioned his memory”\n- **Publisher/date:** Associated Press; 8 February 2024\n- **Source type/confidence:** reputable wire/media summarizing an official report; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Special Counsel Hur found evidence Biden willfully retained and disclosed classified information after leaving office, but concluded charges were not warranted because intent could not be proved beyond a reasonable doubt and innocent explanations could not be excluded. Biden cooperated with the inquiry; the report identified material distinctions from Trump’s alleged obstruction.\n- **Locator:** Opening paragraphs; sections explaining prosecutorial decision and Trump/Biden distinctions.\n- **Period fit:** investigation covered 2023 cooperation and earlier handling; report is post-period retrospective evidence.\n- **Attribution:** direct personal conduct.\n- **Contrary:** No criminal charge; cooperation and evidentiary uncertainty are material mitigation.\n- **Mappings:** 7B.2, 7B.3, 7B.6, 7B.7, 7B.10; 8B.4, 8B.9.\n\n### E018 — Biden’s false and misleading response to Hur report\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.factcheck.org/2024/02/bidens-claims-about-special-counsel-report-on-classified-documents-investigation/|summary-and-willful-retention-sections|Biden-misstated-Hur-findings`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Biden’s Claims About Special Counsel Report on Classified Documents Investigation”\n- **Publisher/date:** FactCheck.org; 9 February 2024\n- **Source type/confidence:** professional fact-checking organization grounded in official report; **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** Biden falsely or misleadingly said the report found a shortage of evidence of willful retention, that no highly classified records were involved, that he did not share classified information with his ghostwriter, and that all home materials were in lockable filing cabinets.\n- **Locator:** Opening bullet list and sections “Willful Retention,” “‘Highly Classified’ Documents,” “Ghostwriter Discussions,” and storage.\n- **Period fit:** post-period response to a report about conduct and investigation active during 2023; use only as retrospective integrity evidence, not as a 2023 speech.\n- **Attribution:** direct personal statements.\n- **Contrary:** Biden correctly emphasized that no charges were warranted and that the report distinguished his cooperation from Trump’s alleged obstruction.\n- **Mappings:** 7B.1, 7B.2, 7B.6, 7B.7, 7B.10.\n\n### E019 — Governing program and implementation portfolio\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.politifact.com/truth-o-meter/promises/biden-promise-tracker/?ruling=true|intro-and-scorecard|Biden-program-tracked-by-verifiable-outcomes`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title:** “Biden Promise Tracker”\n- **Publisher/date:** PolitiFact; continuously updated\n- **Source type/confidence:** fact-checking/project tracker; **medium**\n- **Claim:** The tracker identifies Biden’s explicit program around COVID control, economic recovery, racial justice, and climate, and evaluates 99 promises by verifiable outcomes rather than effort or intent.\n- **Locator:** Introductory paragraphs and top-priority section.\n- **Period fit:** broad tenure context, not a 2023-only inventory\n- **Attribution:** direct campaign/program attribution.\n- **Limitations:** The fetched page did not expose the full 2023 scorecard or individual outcomes cleanly; use as portfolio-identification context only.\n- **Mappings:** 8B.1, 8B.7, 8B.10; contextual 5B.1 and 6B.1.\n\n### E020 — Afghanistan review exposed accountability and adaptation concerns\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/biden-report-afghanistan-withdrawal-blames-trump-2023-04-06/|search-result-summary|Biden-Afghanistan-review-blamed-inherited-constraints`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title:** “Biden Afghanistan report mostly blames Trump for chaotic withdrawal”\n- **Publisher/date:** Reuters; 6–7 April 2023\n- **Source type/confidence:** reputable wire; normally **medium-high**\n- **Claim:** A 2023 administration review argued Biden’s withdrawal choices were severely constrained by conditions inherited from Trump, while critics viewed the review as avoiding accountability for the chaotic execution.\n- **Locator:** Reuters discovery snippet only; direct fetch blocked.\n- **Period fit:** exact 2023 review of 2021 conduct\n- **Attribution:** relevant to Biden’s correction/accountability response, but not a 2023 operational event.\n- **Limitations:** **gateway_only** and retrospective; not counted as final evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.9; 7B.2, 7B.8; 8B.8, 8B.9.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Chapter-by-chapter handoff\n\n## Chapter 1B — Nuclear and existential-risk responsibility\n\n### Local baseline\n\nNo local 1B fact was supplied. All ten 1B dispositions are genuine mapped-field gaps under reason R001, not evidence of safety or responsibility. No extraction error was reported.\n\n### Retained evidence and reuse\n\n- **E001:** concrete 2023 administration offer to observe New START central limits reciprocally, enter post-2026 talks with Russia, engage China without preconditions, and pursue P5 launch notification, crisis communication, human-in-loop control, and transparency.\n- **E002:** material implementation counterpoint—no formal written proposal had been sent three months later, though NSC said private communications occurred.\n- **E003:** possible return of a U.S. nuclear mission to RAF Lakenheath is posture-expansion context and a possible adverse signal, but direct Biden attribution is weak.\n- **E006:** expert interpretation that Biden’s great-power conduct was calm and de-escalatory, with acknowledged hesitation and mixed results.\n\n### Lens coverage\n\n- **Well covered:** 1B.1, 1B.4, 1B.8, 1B.9.\n- **Partly covered:** 1B.2, 1B.3, 1B.7, 1B.10.\n- **Weak:** 1B.5 and 1B.6; no strong opened source directly addressed nuclear shielding of conventional aggression or ally proliferation restraint in 2023.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- 4 mapped source-claim units.\n- 2 defensible source organizations plus Brookings interpretation; official White House policy is embedded through ACA.\n- Source concentration is high: two items share Arms Control Association material.\n- Below the five-unit goal because command-and-control, proliferation, and end-posture technical sources were not sufficiently opened.\n\n### Strongest contrary evidence\n\nThe administration’s detailed arms-control rhetoric did not promptly become a formal written proposal, and possible Lakenheath preparations cut against a simple risk-reduction narrative.\n\n### Unresolved gaps\n\n- Direct evidence of Biden’s personal nuclear-risk decisions.\n- Exact 2023 arsenal/posture change, command-and-control safety, alert policy, and launch authority.\n- Iran, North Korea, Saudi, South Korean, and allied-proliferation choices.\n- Implementation outcomes of AI, biological, cyber, and space safeguards.\n- Independent technical assessment of whether end-2023 posture was safer than the start of 2023.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 2B — International peace, restraint, and lawful security\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF001–LF004 establish unusually large military expenditures but do not show aggression, peacefulness, civilian protection, or Biden’s reasons. They are context only.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **E004:** U.S. veto of a Security Council humanitarian-pauses resolution and its stated self-defense/direct-diplomacy rationale.\n- **E005:** HRW’s mixed finding—support for sanctions and international justice, alongside military assistance to rights-abusing states.\n- **E006:** generally calm great-power management and major support for Ukraine, but slow weapons decisions and mixed policy.\n- **E001:** arms-control diplomacy as a risk-reduction mechanism.\n- **L001:** military-spending context.\n\nReuters discovery additionally identified December 2023 reporting that the administration kept weapons flowing to Israel despite mounting civilian deaths while attempting to pressure Israel privately. Because direct Reuters access was blocked, that candidate is not counted as opened final evidence.\n\n### Lens coverage\n\n- **Covered:** 2B.1, 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.7, 2B.8, 2B.10.\n- **Partial:** 2B.2, 2B.3, 2B.6, 2B.9.\n- No evidence suggests Biden initiated a 2023 war, but absence is not retained as proof.\n- Gaza supplies the central adverse case; Ukraine supplies the main defensive-security and restraint context.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- 5 defensible mapped units including one local context unit.\n- Organizations: UN, HRW, Brookings, ACA, SIPRI local prior.\n- Types: intergovernmental official record, NGO, policy research, primary policy speech, structured dataset.\n- Reasonably diverse but not saturated for civilian-harm and arms-recipient accountability.\n\n### Strongest contrary evidence\n\nThe U.S. vetoed a humanitarian-pauses resolution and continued military assistance in a context of mounting civilian harm. The U.S. explanation stressed Israel’s self-defense, direct diplomacy, hostage release, and civilian protection.\n\n### Unresolved gaps\n\n- Precise 2023 arms-transfer decisions to Israel and Saudi Arabia.\n- Biden’s personal response to civilian-harm warnings.\n- Evidence on Ukraine targeting restrictions, cluster munitions, ATACMS, F-16s, prisoner treatment, and accountability.\n- China/Taiwan coercive-diplomacy and crisis-channel decisions after the balloon incident.\n- Reliable assessment of whether direct diplomacy caused the November Gaza pause/hostage releases.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 3B — Domestic safety, restraint, and protection\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF005–LF010 give country-year physical-integrity and repression measures but cannot separate federal, state, local, or presidential responsibility. They are strong condition priors, not proof of Biden’s conduct.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **E007:** federal use-of-force, duty-to-intervene, profiling, and militarized-equipment reforms; incomplete reach and loopholes.\n- **E008:** increased ICE detention and 90.8% private-facility reliance.\n- **E009:** asylum restrictions exposing migrants to detention, removal, and danger.\n- **E011:** federal attention to threats against election officials and commercial-spyware restrictions.\n- **E014:** incarceration, overdose, and racial disparities, with harm-reduction and naloxone counterevidence.\n- **L002:** structured physical-integrity context.\n\n### Lens coverage\n\n- **Strong:** 3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.5, 3B.7, 3B.10.\n- **Partial:** 3B.3, 3B.6, 3B.8, 3B.9.\n- Evidence distinguishes affirmative federal police reforms from continued immigration-detention and asylum harms.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- 6 mapped units.\n- Organizations: ACLU, HRW, local V-Dem/PTS.\n- Types: NGO documentation and structured datasets.\n- Source-family diversity is below target because authoritative DOJ, DHS OIG, court, or UN treaty-body reports were not successfully extracted.\n\n### Strongest contrary evidence\n\nPolice-reform steps were concrete and federally attributable, but significant profiling loopholes remained and immigration detention expanded markedly.\n\n### Unresolved gaps\n\n- 2023 federal protest treatment and surveillance.\n- DOJ pattern-or-practice investigations and consent decrees with precise outcomes.\n- Federal prison deaths, solitary confinement, and detention conditions.\n- Biden’s personal rhetoric on migrants, protesters, political opponents, and vulnerable groups.\n- Physical-safety nexus for anti-LGBT or reproductive-rights developments.\n- Degree of federal control over state/local police abuse.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 4B — Political freedom versus authoritarian rule\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF011–LF025 provide a strong democratic country baseline: high electoral-democracy, expression, association, judicial/legislative constraint, suffrage, and rule-of-law values, with weaker press-freedom rank. These are inherited/country conditions and cannot alone justify a favorable ruler-attribution claim.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **E010:** elections and courts remained meaningful checks, while state voter restrictions, electoral-college distortions, and disinformation impaired equality.\n- **E011:** federal attention to election-worker threats and spyware; court protection of voting rights.\n- **E007:** federal profiling/use-of-force reforms relevant to equal civic participation.\n- **E009:** restrictive asylum policy relevant to excluded-group equality.\n- **L003:** structured democratic baseline.\n\n### Lens coverage\n\n- **Covered:** 4B.1, 4B.4, 4B.7, 4B.9, 4B.10.\n- **Partial:** 4B.2, 4B.3, 4B.6.\n- **Weak:** 4B.5 and 4B.8. No 2023 transfer-of-power event occurred, and no strong evidence of personality cult, loyalty tests, or state-party capture was found.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- 5 mapped units.\n- Organizations: International IDEA, HRW, ACLU, V-Dem/Freedom House/RSF local priors.\n- Types: intergovernmental analysis, NGO reporting, structured datasets.\n- Federal/state attribution remains the key limiting factor.\n\n### Strongest contrary evidence\n\nThe democratic baseline remained robust and courts operated independently, but voting restrictions and disinformation persisted, mostly through state-level actors outside direct presidential control.\n\n### Unresolved gaps\n\n- Biden’s response to adverse court rulings and congressional defeats.\n- Treatment of hostile media, satire, protests, and congressional investigations.\n- Federal voting-rights enforcement outcomes.\n- CISA/social-media controversy and whether federal anti-disinformation contacts crossed into improper pressure.\n- Direct evidence of self-binding when courts, Congress, or oversight constrained Biden.\n- 2023 press-access and transparency record.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 5B — Economic well-being and prosperity\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF026–LF028 show a wealthy, very large economy in 2023 but say nothing directly about distribution, macro stewardship, policy implementation, or Biden’s contribution.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **E013:** official poverty declined while supplemental and child poverty rose.\n- **E014:** HRW synthesis of rising inequality, racial wealth disparity, and expired social support.\n- **E016:** jobs and infrastructure had a factual core, but Biden overstated comparability or causality in some public claims.\n- **E012:** BLS real-wage improvement is a promising official fact but remains gateway-only pending direct locator capture.\n- **E015:** SAVE plan is a promising direct policy item but gateway-only.\n- **L004:** GDP and population context.\n- **E019:** explicit policy portfolio context.\n\n### Lens coverage\n\n- **Covered:** 5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.6, 5B.7, 5B.8, 5B.9, 5B.10.\n- **Partial:** 5B.2, 5B.4, 5B.5.\n- No strong retained source evaluates economic appointments, competition policy, favoritism, or procurement capture in 2023.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- 5 defensible units if counting E019 and local context; E012/E015 excluded from strict count pending underlying locators.\n- Organizations: Census, HRW, PolitiFact, World Bank local prior.\n- Types: official statistics, NGO, fact-checking media, structured dataset.\n- Policy implementation evidence is thinner than outcome evidence.\n\n### Strongest contrary evidence\n\nLabor-market and real-wage signs improved, but supplemental and child poverty worsened, and some presidential claims overstated causality or historical comparison.\n\n### Unresolved gaps\n\n- 2023 inflation path and Federal Reserve versus fiscal-policy attribution.\n- Implementation status of IRA, CHIPS, and infrastructure investments.\n- Fiscal-deficit and debt trajectory with CBO analysis.\n- Distribution across income, race, sex, and region.\n- Antitrust/competition outcomes and labor-market regulation.\n- Personnel quality and independence at Treasury, NEC, OMB, and regulatory agencies.\n- Evidence of policy correction after inflation and supply-chain problems.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 6B — Social well-being and human development\n\n### Local baseline\n\nThe supplied package contained no 6B facts. All ten dispositions are R001 mapped-field gaps, not evidence that social welfare was unavailable or unchanged. There were no extraction-error IDs.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **E013:** official and supplemental poverty trends, including higher child poverty.\n- **E014:** overdose harms, racial disparities, harm-reduction investment, and naloxone access.\n- **E008/E009:** immigration detention and asylum harms affecting vulnerable migrants.\n- **E007:** police-policy reforms relevant to safety and dignity.\n- **E015:** SAVE repayment plan is context pending a direct underlying locator.\n\n### Lens coverage\n\n- **Covered:** 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.6, 6B.8, 6B.10.\n- **Partial:** 6B.1, 6B.4, 6B.5, 6B.7, 6B.9.\n- The evidence is concentrated on poverty, drugs, policing, immigration, and education debt; it is sparse on health-insurance access, schools, housing, water, food, and institutional durability.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- 5 defensible cross-chapter units, but only two are direct welfare-outcome sources.\n- Organizations: Census, HRW, ACLU.\n- Types: official statistics and NGO reporting.\n- The chapter is not saturated; it remains below normal evidence depth for a prominent contemporary ruler.\n\n### Strongest contrary evidence\n\nThe administration expanded harm-reduction tools and policing safeguards, but child SPM poverty rose and migrant detention/asylum policies imposed significant harms.\n\n### Unresolved gaps\n\n- 2023 uninsured rate, ACA enrollment, Medicaid unwinding, and avoidable coverage loss.\n- Housing affordability and homelessness.\n- SNAP emergency-allotment expiration and food insecurity.\n- School achievement, pandemic recovery, and childcare.\n- Disaster response in Maui and other 2023 crises.\n- Maternal mortality, reproductive care, and rural access.\n- Program delivery and durability beyond announcements.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 7B — Personal integrity and honesty\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF029–LF032, LF011, and LF024 indicate relatively favorable national corruption and rule-of-law context. They cannot prove Biden’s personal integrity and are not treated as such.\n\n### Retained ruler-specific evidence\n\n- **E017:** evidence of improper classified-material retention/disclosure, prosecutorial uncertainty, no charge, cooperation, and distinctions from obstruction.\n- **E018:** direct false or misleading statements by Biden about the special counsel’s findings.\n- **E016:** a mixed pattern of accurate claims, contextual exaggeration, and unsupported examples in the 2023 State of the Union.\n- **E020:** the Afghanistan review as a gateway-only accountability candidate.\n- **L005:** country corruption context only.\n\n### Lens coverage\n\n- **Strong:** 7B.1, 7B.2, 7B.6, 7B.10.\n- **Partial:** 7B.3, 7B.7, 7B.8.\n- **Weak:** 7B.4, 7B.5, 7B.9. No opened final source established Biden’s personal enrichment, family benefit through state action, crony appointments, or nepotistic procurement.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- 4 defensible claim units including local context; below the minimum target.\n- Organizations: AP, FactCheck.org, PolitiFact, local Transparency/V-Dem.\n- Types: media, fact-checking, structured datasets.\n- Evidence is concentrated in one scandal family. This concentration must not be mistaken for a complete integrity pattern.\n\n### Strongest contrary evidence\n\nThe classified-documents case contains both adverse and mitigating facts: improper retention/disclosure evidence and misleading public responses, but no criminal charge, extensive cooperation, and no reported obstruction comparable to Trump’s case.\n\n### Unresolved gaps\n\n- Hunter Biden and other family-business dealings: evidence of Joe Biden’s knowledge, benefit, or intervention versus unsupported partisan allegations.\n- Financial disclosures, gifts, beneficial ownership, procurement, and campaign finance.\n- Appointment competence versus donor/loyalist influence.\n- Treatment of inspectors general, whistleblowers, congressional subpoenas, and press scrutiny.\n- Pattern-level analysis of corrections and factual candor beyond selected speeches.\n- Independent evidence of costly candor or strengthened ethics institutions.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 8B — Effectiveness and competence\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF011 accountability and LF024 rule of law are inherited state-capacity context only. They do not establish Biden’s program, execution, adaptation, or outcomes.\n\n### Frozen provisional portfolio\n\nEvidence supports a broad explicit portfolio rather than a cherry-picked goal:\n\n1. Economic recovery and worker-centered industrial policy.\n2. Infrastructure, semiconductors, climate, and supply-chain investment.\n3. COVID/public-health recovery and social protection.\n4. Racial justice and policing reform.\n5. Alliance restoration, Ukraine support, and strategic competition.\n6. Immigration/border administration.\n7. Democracy protection.\n8. Necessary core-government management.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **E019:** program identification from a 99-promise tracker.\n- **E007:** policing order translated into 20-plus policies and guidance, with incomplete implementation.\n- **E002:** arms-control strategy suffered a formalization/follow-through gap.\n- **E006:** substantial Ukraine mobilization but repeated delay in weapons decisions.\n- **E008:** immigration detention policy moved substantially in a direction inconsistent with Biden’s earlier private-prison promise, while demonstrating operational continuation rather than implementation of the stated reform.\n- **E013/E016:** mixed outcomes and some overclaiming.\n- **E017:** classified-record handling and memory/management concerns, with cooperation as mitigation.\n- **E020:** Afghanistan review is a gateway-only adaptation/accountability candidate.\n\n### Lens coverage\n\n- **Covered:** 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.9, 8B.10.\n- **Partial:** 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.8.\n- **Weak:** 8B.4; there is insufficient sourced evidence about personnel selection and empowerment across the portfolio.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- 8 cross-mapped units.\n- Organizations: PolitiFact, ACLU, ACA, Brookings, Census, AP.\n- Types: tracker/fact-checking, NGO implementation critique, specialist policy analysis, official outcomes, reputable media.\n- This chapter has enough evidence to identify real implementation successes and failures but remains thin on budgets, audits, milestones, and causal attribution.\n\n### Strongest contrary evidence\n\nThe administration converted some goals into substantial policies and mobilization, especially policing rules and Ukraine support, but arms-control follow-through lagged, immigration detention contradicted a prior commitment, and several outcomes had mixed or external causes.\n\n### Unresolved gaps\n\n- Comprehensive implementation status for IRA, CHIPS, infrastructure, and climate programs.\n- Border-processing performance and adaptation.\n- Personnel turnover, coordination, and cabinet-management evidence.\n- Budget execution and GAO/OIG findings.\n- Measurable 2023 milestones across the frozen portfolio.\n- Durability and causal separation from Congress, the Federal Reserve, states, and inherited capacity.\n- Direct evidence of Biden replacing ineffective implementers or reallocating resources after failure.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Discovery queries and candidate outcomes\n\nRepresentative provenance-preserving queries included:\n\n- `Joe Biden 2023 presidency major policies Ukraine nuclear arms economy health democracy ethics accomplishments failures`\n- `Joe Biden 2023 nuclear arms control New START risk reduction China official Reuters`\n- `site:armscontrol.org Biden 2023 nuclear arms control New START Sullivan speech`\n- `site:fas.org Biden 2023 nuclear weapons arsenal policy`\n- `Biden 2023 Ukraine Israel Gaza diplomacy civilian harm arms Reuters UN`\n- `site:un.org Biden Gaza 2023 ceasefire veto civilian protection`\n- `Biden administration 2023 police accountability immigration human rights ACLU DOJ`\n- `site:hrw.org United States 2023 Biden human rights immigration police`\n- `Biden 2023 democracy voting rights press freedom political institutions Reuters AP`\n- `Biden economy 2023 inflation jobs wages IRA CHIPS implementation CBO GAO Reuters`\n- `site:bls.gov 2023 employment inflation real wages annual United States`\n- `site:census.gov 2023 child poverty health insurance Biden 2022 report`\n- `Biden social welfare 2023 healthcare Medicaid child poverty student loans homelessness Reuters HHS`\n- `Joe Biden 2023 ethics integrity classified documents Hunter investigation fact check Reuters AP`\n- `site:politifact.com Joe Biden 2023 false claims fact check`\n- `Biden 2023 effectiveness competence implementation agenda Afghanistan border inflation Reuters Brookings`\n\n### Rejected or non-final candidates\n\n1. **Reuters articles:** discovered but direct fetch blocked by robots. Kept only as gateway context unless independently opened elsewhere.\n2. **CRS R47828:** relevant to Gaza and U.S. policy but HTTP 403; rejected as inaccessible in this run.\n3. **DOJ Hur PDF:** canonical and highly valuable, but fetch produced raw PDF bytes. AP and FactCheck.org provided usable, attributed summaries. The PDF itself should be extracted by the parent if possible.\n4. **Brookings Sullivan PDF:** raw PDF bytes; not retained as a precise extracted item.\n5. **GAO financial-statement PDFs:** search snippets were too broad and lacked precise program-level locators.\n6. **DOJ OIG management-challenges page:** fetch returned only generic site text; rejected for no usable claim.\n7. **White House live pages:** search was polluted by post-2025 content. The archived/reproduced Sullivan speech was used instead.\n8. **Wikipedia:** discovery/orientation only; no final evidence retained.\n9. **AEI and partisan committee pages:** not used because more direct or less interested sources were available.\n10. **PolitiFact Promise Tracker full scorecard:** usable only to identify the program because the fetched page did not expose a clean complete outcome list.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Local-data audit\n\n## Retained as context\n\n- **LF001–LF004:** retained together as L001 for 2B military-spending context.\n- **LF005–LF010:** retained together as L002 for 3B condition context.\n- **LF011–LF025:** retained together as L003 for 4B democratic/institutional context. LF011 and LF024 also reused as inherited context in 7B/8B.\n- **LF026–LF028:** retained together as L004 for 5B economic scale.\n- **LF029–LF032:** retained together as L005 for 7B corruption context.\n\n## Used as direct ruler evidence\n\nNone. Every local item measures country-year conditions or capacity, not Biden’s personal conduct.\n\n## Left unused as a direct lens answer\n\nAll local facts were left unused as direct answers where their candidate mappings exceeded what the field actually measures. In particular:\n\n- Military spending was not treated as aggression.\n- V-Dem/PTS safety fields were not treated as a Biden order or remedy.\n- Democratic indices were not treated as proof of self-binding.\n- GDP was not treated as policy competence or distributional success.\n- Corruption indices were not personalized into honesty.\n- Accountability/rule-of-law fields were not converted into effectiveness.\n\n## Empty local chapters\n\n- **1B:** no supplied facts; this is an R001 local-field gap.\n- **6B:** no supplied facts; this is an R001 local-field gap.\n- Neither gap is evidence of zero activity, irrelevance, responsibility, or failure.\n\n## Data-quality errors\n\n`error_methodology_ids` was empty. There is therefore no distinct local extraction failure to report.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Self-audit and outstanding cautions\n\n## Temporal fit\n\n- E001–E011 and E013–E016 primarily establish 2023 acts or conditions.\n- E006, E013, E014, E017, and E018 were published after 2023 but directly assess or report target-period facts.\n- E018 concerns a February 2024 statement responding to an investigation active in 2023; it is retrospective integrity evidence and must not be silently treated as a 2023 statement.\n- E020 concerns a 2023 review of a 2021 event and is only context.\n- Reuters gateway items must not be promoted without opening the underlying source.\n\n## Attribution\n\nStrongest Biden/executive attribution:\n\n- E001 arms-control program\n- E004 U.S. veto and diplomacy\n- E007 policing executive-order implementation\n- E008/E009 immigration policy\n- E016 direct public claims\n- E017/E018 classified-document conduct and statements\n\nWeak or shared attribution:\n\n- State voting restrictions\n- Court decisions\n- Poverty and wage outcomes\n- Military expenditures\n- National corruption and democracy scores\n- Ukraine aid, which depended heavily on Congress\n- State/local policing and incarceration\n\n## Weak/context-only material\n\n- All local priors.\n- E012 pending direct BLS locator.\n- E015 Reuters gateway.\n- E019 portfolio identification rather than implementation judgment.\n- E020 Reuters gateway and retrospective event.\n\n## Claims needing independent corroboration\n\n- ACLU’s immigration-detention statistics should ideally be checked against ICE/TRAC primary records.\n- HRW’s assessment of the asylum rule should be paired with the Federal Register rule, court findings, or DHS OIG evidence.\n- Brookings’s de-escalation interpretation should be balanced with technical or diplomatic records.\n- Arms-control implementation claims should be paired with State Department records and independent FAS/SIPRI analysis.\n- Gaza arms-recipient responsibility requires opened transfer records and civilian-harm assessments.\n- Classified-document findings should ultimately cite precise Hur report pages rather than summaries alone.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Manifest accounting index\n\nThis compact index can be persisted as `research-ledger-manifest.json` and expanded with rejected candidates:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": \"ruler_research_ledger_manifest_v1\",\n  \"entries\": [\n    {\"provisional_id\":\"L001\",\"canonical_fact_key\":\"local-prior:2B.1|LF001-LF004|USA-2023-military-spending\",\"disposition\":\"context\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\":\"L002\",\"canonical_fact_key\":\"local-prior:3B.1|LF005-LF010|USA-2023-physical-integrity\",\"disposition\":\"context\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\":\"L003\",\"canonical_fact_key\":\"local-prior:4B.1|LF011-LF025|USA-2023-democracy-freedom\",\"disposition\":\"context\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\":\"L004\",\"canonical_fact_key\":\"local-prior:5B.1|LF026-LF028|USA-2023-economic-level\",\"disposition\":\"context\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\":\"L005\",\"canonical_fact_key\":\"local-prior:7B.1|LF029-LF032|USA-2023-corruption\",\"disposition\":\"context\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\":\"E001\",\"canonical_fact_key\":\"https://www.armscontrol.org/events/2023-06/remarks-national-security-advisor-jake-sullivan-arms-control-association-aca-annual|sections-US-ready-engage-Russia-China-P5|Biden-administration-arms-control-offer\",\"disposition\":\"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\":\"E002\",\"canonical_fact_key\":\"https://www.armscontrol.org/blog/2023-09/nuclear-disarmament-monitor|opening-paragraphs-US-proposal|no-formal-written-proposal-three-months\",\"disposition\":\"fi